#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119618 :question/id #uuid "13ebde04-8518-5837-9420-c9487debc562" :question/math-academy-id "e-4791" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c_1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{c_2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then what is $x_1+x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "If $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1} &=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $x_1=5,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ 4(5)+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(5)+(-15) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15,$ which means\n\n$$\n5 \\mathbf{c_1}-15 \\mathbf{c_2} = \\mathbf{d}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2=5-15=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119662 :question/id #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" :question/math-academy-id "e-5417" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119663 :question/id #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5418" :question/problem "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119665 :question/id #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-6143" :question/problem "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & - 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & - 1 \\\\ - 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\quad\nY=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}2 & - 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119666 :question/id #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" :question/math-academy-id "e-6148" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5.$ What is the value of $\\det(2A)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119669 :question/id #uuid "d9beff9d-db58-540f-811e-a07fb77d3d92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6191" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a_2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a_3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b,$ then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3.\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119672 :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]},$ $\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]},$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}15 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}15 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119673 :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257" :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_4={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{rblack}x_2} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} + {\\color{rblack}x_3} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}  = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_4}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n$$\n(-5) \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + 0 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} + 7 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}  = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9a750e3-d153-524f-a54e-d722127369f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448404} {:answer/id #uuid "474d3371-8932-5041-9ec0-7a2713e5c329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a750e3-d153-524f-a54e-d722127369f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448404} :answer-field/id #uuid "6751ef52-bd73-517e-81c4-c56485295df6" :answer-field/key "$2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449242} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6a2d4a-341d-5cb7-9f08-a6c31b8bf13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448406} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f1e515-ad10-5a3e-8ac9-3eaf8f191a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f1e515-ad10-5a3e-8ac9-3eaf8f191a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448407} :answer-field/id #uuid "4776a07a-b85f-5a19-a13c-2087f8f5df16" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0094a7-d9e0-57fc-84a4-bfce73870162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448408} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad95581-5f03-5403-ae4d-5a62d3cbd6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad95581-5f03-5403-ae4d-5a62d3cbd6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99b2aa8-b962-5194-be9e-f3d0a51b7833" :answer-field/key "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ec28c6e-377f-591a-9037-3511702a70c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-155965" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$ is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-3+1 \\\\ -2-0+2 \\\\ 4-0+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement “The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$”; “The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b24b9fb-3a78-5b39-97db-5d260de94782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448410} {:answer/id #uuid "f64cc72e-31d2-5bec-9c8f-199c1ae77a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b24b9fb-3a78-5b39-97db-5d260de94782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448410} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc2eb58e-ea01-5c50-b62f-9127356e961b" :answer-field/key "${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a16cea7-d0c4-5953-9641-00bd84983fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448412} {:answer/id #uuid "3fafa4a0-feb2-53eb-9c91-01bf033b7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fafa4a0-feb2-53eb-9c91-01bf033b7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448413} :answer-field/id #uuid "90edb75f-056d-51fe-b4d6-0959b7052573" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abdbc03-a454-57f6-bb48-be031db06b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448414} {:answer/id #uuid "20dc8704-4e53-5add-b192-596188f94535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20dc8704-4e53-5add-b192-596188f94535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448415} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee14665-3f3f-5d5c-bf70-8e4c97353be2" :answer-field/key "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62e09db0-cf5e-5972-a5d2-6baa771c45b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-155967" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$ is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement “The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$”; “The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88045f96-4e5c-56f2-aa3d-094165238e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448416} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e3a90-b6f1-5944-9c06-c0858870b2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e3a90-b6f1-5944-9c06-c0858870b2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448417} :answer-field/id #uuid "592d5ef9-838c-58f7-b2e0-f8df0b00d16c" :answer-field/key "${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uu