columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00939ade-063c-559f-bd8d-433b0e5ab9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319394} {:answer/id #uuid "0660261a-521f-50ee-a7a8-84d6b48be7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319408} {:answer/id #uuid "212bd7b4-020a-5096-b846-a43a66bd1476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319494} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0a4c71-c2ad-546d-bd08-c8f07a432b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319676} {:answer/id #uuid "e7669353-a89f-52b0-8d7f-e2dbd80728f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00939ade-063c-559f-bd8d-433b0e5ab9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319394} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84f3f9c-bbd2-5136-9f5b-023eb366b3d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa75430a-c185-59a6-ae3f-a174dc3e1bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35290" :question/problem "If \n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then $\\text{rank}(H)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $H,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(H)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cfb642-9e64-528d-927d-c6e8ff6db964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321323} {:answer/id #uuid "1c284991-45e3-5538-8aa1-f55c069004b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321361} {:answer/id #uuid "b989efb8-be23-5a75-ad11-9e6cd939ad34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322587} {:answer/id #uuid "e3edb4e2-9f7d-5526-9df5-1b6e4a177ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322933} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5a983-9153-55a4-b046-e874770016c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b989efb8-be23-5a75-ad11-9e6cd939ad34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322587} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5fe3d3d-94be-516c-9d55-e5600444b9f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fe4864a-ed27-50fc-8816-ad1ec4b0dfc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35694" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ ${a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37511006-3cc1-5044-a452-04788f70a6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324422} {:answer/id #uuid "52ff4a1e-e084-5b7a-9da2-3d3878cd71ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324653} {:answer/id #uuid "71e11479-c1c3-5398-9ce8-e2748445bd42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324913} {:answer/id #uuid "9d025d13-dbb9-5f4d-81e6-a2fb2be38c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325304} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d34638-1f12-5d11-897f-c363aca5a5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d025d13-dbb9-5f4d-81e6-a2fb2be38c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325304} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa554bb6-6241-52a2-a8a6-09b04c9e8078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d4250d6-4c9c-5a2c-ac92-71ea5c8419b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42076" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e01723b-cc89-5e3f-b933-6517b2094770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409662} {:answer/id #uuid "91ff05b8-c923-5cbc-9974-2858e86d4877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409672} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b3cea5-79ed-5121-8909-27b79eb945a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409674} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a98397-80e5-5a87-b4ed-d166d96908c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409675} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7338ba-732f-5bfb-aded-530b4a5f8d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7338ba-732f-5bfb-aded-530b4a5f8d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc16361c-3306-5054-a003-e31388cab6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a9d752b-69e4-5f21-888f-3429a8443f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42503" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
